00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.829 align:start position:0%
so before 1920  children young people who were diagnosed  with type 1 diabetes  had only days weeks months to live  and very famous clinicians like  dr jocelyn in the united states found  ways to make these people live a little  longer  a few months sometimes a few years  by treating them with harsh diets  avoiding  carbohydrate intake for instance they  were called ketogenic diets  and by doing that people survived they  didn't  really live they just survived they lost  all their weight all their muscle  all their fat tissue and so it was  really a  very very grim prognosis before the  discovery of insulin  in 1921 it was like different things  came together  in a very lucky way with on the one hand  an orthopedic surgeon returning from the  war  frederick banting who wanted to do  research  who contacted professor mcleod at the  university of toronto  interested in physiology and so mcleod  gave  banting some lab space and also  allocated a student to him  charles best and he asked him to work on  these  pancreatic extracts they started working  on dogs  and what they showed is indeed that if  they  made extracts from pancreases they could  treat  pancreatectomized dogs and keep them  alive however their extracts were  impure and that is where everything came  together when they met  dr kolip who was a pharmacist  who was able to develop a method to make  purified extracts this time not from  dogs  but from cow pancreases from the  slaughter house  and so it was at the end of 1921  that kolip was certain that his  extract was pure enough to allow  experiments in humans  and so it was on the 10th of january  1922 that  a young boy leonard thompson who was a  14 year old child  dying from diabetes was treated with  insulin  for the first time in a toronto hospital  very soon the university of toronto also  realized that they couldn't  really manufacture um the the amounts of  insulin that were needed to to treat all  the people  living with diabetes and desperate to  get their hands on this miracle drug  and so they started to collaborate with  um industry like the conaut laboratories  and then  soon after with other bigger  pharmaceutical companies not only in  north america  but also very soon in  europe very soon people started  altering the nature of the insulin they  were using to treat  patients with diabetes the first thing  they tried was to  make the need for injections of insulin  every four to six hours  less and so they tried to make the  insulin longer  acting and some of my colleagues call  that  the dark ages of insulin treatment in  people with diabetes because  treating them only with a very  long-acting  insulin took us even further  from the physiology of what a beta cell  makes  as an insulin profile and so in the 40s  50s 60s  70s people being treated with just one  or two injections of insulin per day  meant that their glucose levels were not  well controlled  at all and so what we discovered is that  bad glucose control  led to severe long-term complications  of diabetes like blindness like kidney  failure  and heart problems  the hunt for manufacturing  different types of insulin purer  insulin less immunogenic insulin  was really aided by the whole genetic  engineering of bacteria and yeast  and therefore these new analogues that  we're now using  is really the fruit of all of that  discovery  the exciting bit about the celebration  of insulin is that the party is not  over yet indeed we are still discovering  new forms of insulin we're now at the  um beginning of being able to  use oral insulin we're seeing  um glucose sensitive acting  insulins we're seeing liver preferred  insulin so  research on insulin is extremely active  but honestly i hope we can stop  researching insulin quite soon and that  we may  find better ways to treat people with  type 2 diabetes  and that we can finally prevent and cure  type 1 diabetes  you
 

